WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.587
PostgreSQL provides ILIKE for case-insensitive text pattern matching.

00:00:03.587 --> 00:00:06.902
The percent sign represents any sequence of characters.

00:00:06.902 --> 00:00:10.647
A pattern such as C followed by percent means starts with C.

00:00:10.647 --> 00:00:13.477
Percent followed by son means ends with son.

00:00:13.477 --> 00:00:17.682
Percent on both sides searches for text appearing anywhere inside the value.

00:00:17.682 --> 00:00:20.652
Run the example and look at the patient names that match.

00:00:20.652 --> 00:00:22.612
Then try changing the search pattern.

00:00:22.612 --> 00:00:25.512
Before each run, predict what kind of names should appear.

00:00:25.512 --> 00:00:28.642
ILIKE is useful when capitalization should not matter.

00:00:28.642 --> 00:00:33.107
LIKE is similar, but case behavior can differ depending on the database and collation.

00:00:33.107 --> 00:00:37.752
Pattern matching is convenient, but broad patterns can also return many rows.

00:00:37.752 --> 00:00:40.837
Use a pattern that reflects the actual search requirement.

00:00:40.837 --> 00:00:43.982
Think of SELECT work as progressively shaping a question.

00:00:43.982 --> 00:00:50.552
First identify the rows and columns you need, then filter, derive expressions, sort, and limit for inspection.

00:00:50.552 --> 00:00:55.047
A useful habit is to test each part on a small result before adding more complexity,

00:00:55.047 --> 00:00:57.520
especially when a filter contains NULL-sensitive logic.
